            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "I agree: **route 2 should lead, with route 1 testing the specific mechanisms we propose.** It addresses the central unresolved issue—controlling adverse stationary curvature near a possible collision. Our shared theta representation and paired integrals give us more structure to work with.\n\nI’ve taken a first step, with two useful findings:\n\n- **Preserving the sign helps.** A certified theta stationary branch in \\(101<x<102\\) has positive signed curvature even where specified stronger norm bounds fail under an alternative centering. This supports the approach, although it adds no height coverage.\n- **Small errors alone are insufficient.** The new square-based sufficient condition forces its leftover residual to vanish at a hypothetical collision. An absolute tail bound cannot establish that cancellation.\n\nThat suggests a focused next objective: find a **theta-specific relation controlling the adverse residual product**, retaining the arithmetic cross terms. Route 1 can then test a few prescribed decompositions on genuine stationary branches, with all tails paid and no loss constant manufactured by dividing by the energy.\n\nI saved the derivation, pilot, and next steps in [Note 21](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-2/active/21_quaternion_theta_geometry/notes/21_ONE_SIDED_STATIONARY_ESTIMATES_AND_THETA_PILOT_20261014.md). Independent checks passed all **14 algebra checks and eight time slabs**. The all-height inequality remains open, but we now have a more precise way to decide which proposed mechanisms deserve further effort."
            ]
